[Student "S"]
{Because you have an invitational hand with a 4-card Major (\Ss) you use Stayman.

After your 2\C bid partner says 2\D.

Partner does not have 4 (\Ss) so you have no 8-card fit there. However, he might have 3 \Hs, so you now bid 2\H. Partner will understand you are showing a 5-card suit.

Partner bids 3\H.  

This could go either way. You started out with 9 points, but perhaps you could revalue your hand upward after you find the fit. The problem with that is those two doubletons are in the long-trump hand, and taking ruffs in the long-trump hand doesn't usually add tricks. You take the conservative route and pass.

North plays 3\H. East leads the \CJ.

<b>North plays 3\H. East leads the \CJ.</b>

Losers: \S 2 : \H 2 : \D 1 : \C 1 : Total = 6

You may be able to ruff 1 \S in dummy, and discard another on a long \D. And you hope to keep your trump losers to 1.

Start by covering the \CJ to set up a sure \C trick. West wins the \CA and returns a \C, won by dummy's \CK. It looks better to start the trumps from your side, so play a \D toward your \DK. East wins and plays a \C, which you ruff. Now lead a \H, playing the \HK if East plays low. Assume this wins the trick. Lead a \H back, finessing the \H9 since you think East must hold the \HA. He does hold it, and takes it right now. East leads a \S. You win the \SA in dummy, play the \S7 to your \SK, and ruff a \S with dummy's last trump. Now lead dummy's \D Q J, discarding a \S on the last one. You have only good trumps left.

The play for this hand was not straightforward at all, and might have taken a different path had the West/East cards been arranged differently. However, the general approach of ruffing a \S in dummy and setting up the \D suit would bring the best result with most arrangements.}
[Play "E"]
CJ

